HOW_TO_READ = ('<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (dashed border = interface; &laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed '
 'list of values). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. Bottom: its methods. <b>The arrows.</b> Hollow triangle = '
 'implements. Filled diamond = owns: the machine owns the inventory, the coin box and the tray, and an inventory owns '
 'its slots. Plain arrow = references: a slot points at a product, the coin box counts coins. Dashed lavender = handed '
 'in through <code>configure()</code> or <code>setClock()</code>. Dotted blue = notifies: the one call the machine makes after it has released the lock. The plain arrows out of the machine are what it returns (an Outcome) and what it publishes (a MachineEvent), and the arrow from HappyHourDiscount back to PricingStrategy is one price rule wrapping another. <b>Where state lives:</b> a '
 'slot has its count; the coin box has how many of each coin; the tray has whatever is waiting to be picked up; the '
 'machine has all three plus the escrow, the mode and the one lock, and it is the only class that writes to more than '
 'one of them. Everything on the right-hand column is handed in and could be replaced without opening the machine. On '
 'the next page this diagram is worth keeping in a second tab while you read the code.')

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ('Read it with page 03 open in a second tab if you want the diagram beside it. The green comment above '
 'each class and method says what it does and what it guarantees; read only those first for the shape, then the '
 'bodies, and start with <code>VendingMachine.select</code>, which is the whole interview in thirty lines. Each copy '
 'button copies that whole file. Below Main.java: Extensions.java (every follow-up\'s reference code, with an '
 '<code>ExtDemo</code> main that runs all of it) and FailureTests.java (eight claims proven; '
 '<code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> prints ALL PASS).')

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3>'
 '<button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div><div class="cb"><div class="prompt">' + PROMPT +
 '</div>Before typing, write your six to eight clarifying questions; then type in the order of Main.java: the enums '
 'and the money (Coin, Product, Slot), the two owners (Inventory, CoinBox), the tray and the outcome, the mode and '
 'action table, the four interfaces with one implementation each, the machine with its lock and the order inside '
 '<code>select</code>, the front panel, and a main with the race.</div></div>')

FU = [
("Happy hour: twenty percent off between four and six, without touching VendingMachine.", "functional", 10,
 "The price is already an interface, so happy hour is one new class that wraps the list price: it asks the wrapped "
 "rule for the price, reads the hour from the handed-in clock, and takes twenty percent off inside the window. The "
 "machine does not change; the only edit is the line where the price rule is handed in. Because the hour comes from "
 "the clock and not from the wall, a test can make any minute of any day four-thirty.",
 sect(src, "final class HappyHourDiscount", "interface ChangeStrategy")),

("Two panels press for the last unit at the same instant. Prove it cannot oversell, with a test.", "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between reading \"A1 has one left\" and writing \"A1 has none\". The whole sale &mdash; the checks, "
 "the change plan, the motor, the commit &mdash; happens inside one lock, so no other panel can run in that gap. The "
 "proof: forty threads wait on one latch, the latch opens, all forty buy the last unit, and the test counts four "
 "things: exactly one item left the machine, the column ended at zero and never below, every one of the thirty-nine "
 "losers got its whole thirty rupees back, and the float grew by exactly one price. The last two assertions are the "
 "money invariant, which is the one most candidates never state.",
 T("// 1. two terminals press", "// 2. the float cannot make change")),

("One lock for the whole machine, with a two-second motor inside it. Does that scale?", "non-functional", 5,
 "Everything except the motor is about one and a half microseconds of lock per sale: a map lookup, one price call, a "
 "change plan over five denominations and ten small writes. The motor is two seconds, and today it runs inside the "
 "lock, which is the honest weak spot &mdash; at three hundred sales a day that is six hundred seconds of lock in "
 "eighty-six thousand, or 0.7% busy, and a real machine has one keypad, so nobody waits. It stops being fine the "
 "moment a bank of machines shares one stock service. Rung one takes the motor out: reserve the unit under a very "
 "short lock, drop with the lock released, then commit or give the reservation back. Rung two is a lock per column. "
 "Rung three lets the count itself decide with a compare-and-set, which is what the database's conditional update "
 "does for a shared service.",
 X("motor outside the lock", "a claim without a lock") + "\n" + X("a claim without a lock", "a mode with behaviour")),

("The motor jams after the money is in. What is the state of the machine, and show me the code.", "functional", 10,
 "Nothing is half done. <code>select</code> proves everything first &mdash; stock, price, and that the change can be "
 "made &mdash; while touching no counts at all, then turns the motor. If the motor returns false or throws, the whole "
 "escrow goes to the tray, the mode goes back to idle, and the column and the float are exactly what they were: not "
 "one unit lost, not one paise moved. Only after the item has fallen does anything commit, and from that point on "
 "every step is a field write that cannot fail. The failure test jams the motor with a one-line lambda and checks all "
 "four numbers afterwards.",
 sect(src, "    Outcome select(String code)", "    /** Coin return")),

("It cannot make change. What happens, can you warn before they pay, and is greedy even the right algorithm?", "functional", 10,
 "The change rule is a pure function: given a copy of the float plus the coins in escrow and the amount owed, it "
 "returns the coins or null. When it returns null the machine refuses the sale, drops the whole escrow into the tray "
 "and goes back to idle, having touched neither the count nor the float. Warning first is the same function asked "
 "earlier: the advisor copies the float, adds the coins the customer is about to pay with, and asks whether the change "
 "could be made &mdash; the \"exact change only\" lamp on a real machine, and safe to run precisely because it mutates "
 "nothing. And no, greedy alone is not right: it is provably optimal only with canonical denominations and unlimited "
 "coins, while a real float is lopsided. Asked for six rupees from one five and three twos it takes the five, cannot "
 "finish, and reports the change impossible &mdash; refusing a sale the machine could have made. The default rule is "
 "greedy that puts a coin back and tries again; for arbitrary denominations the drop-in is a bounded subset-sum over "
 "the coins actually in the float, one new class behind the same interface.",
 X("exact change lamp", "bounded change DP") + "\n" + X("bounded change DP", "hold and pay")),

("Take a card, not just coins.", "twist", 10,
 "The order is the same, with one swap: the card charge becomes the irreversible step instead of the motor. Check "
 "the column has stock, charge the card, turn the motor, and only then take the unit off the coil. A card brings one "
 "case coins cannot: the money moved and then the motor jammed, and for that there is a compensating refund back to "
 "the card, after which the stock is untouched and the customer is whole. The escrow, the coin box and the change "
 "rule are not involved at all, which is why this is a new class and not an edit.",
 X("card payment", "exact change lamp")),

("\"Hold my Coke, I will pay in thirty seconds.\"", "twist", 10,
 "A hold takes the unit out of stock the moment it is made, so nobody else can buy it, and it carries the time it "
 "expires, computed from the injected clock. Every entry point sweeps first: any hold whose time has passed puts its "
 "unit straight back into the column. Paying a live hold just removes it; paying an expired one returns false, and "
 "the unit is already back on sale. Because time is handed in, the test moves the clock thirty-one seconds forward "
 "instead of sleeping.",
 X("hold and pay", "operator console")),

("Persist the float and the counts. Then: a bank of machines shares one stock service.", "twist", 5,
 "The counts and the float go behind a repository interface with save and load, and the in-memory implementation is "
 "all a test needs; the sale path does not change, because the machine still writes the same maps under the same "
 "lock. For a shared service the interesting line is the decrement: what is a lock-protected <code>qty--</code> here "
 "becomes <code>UPDATE slot SET qty = qty - 1 WHERE code = ? AND qty &gt; 0</code>, and zero rows updated means "
 "another terminal got the last one. That is the database's compare-and-set, and it is the same idea as the lock: "
 "one atomic step decides the winner.",
 X("persistence", "motor outside the lock")),

("The operator dashboard polls counts and cash a thousand times a second and wants low-stock alerts. No slowing the sale.", "non-functional", 5,
 "Both halves are already there. The reads are O(1) by construction: a column's count is one map lookup, the float's "
 "worth is a sum over five denominations, and nothing anywhere counts or scans, at any poll rate. Each read does take "
 "the machine's lock, for nanoseconds, so a count is never torn by a sale in flight. The alerts are listeners on the "
 "one small event every commit publishes after the lock is released, inside a try/catch: the pager records a column "
 "that hit zero, the low-cash watcher a float under its floor, a telemetry sink the sales per column. The dashboard "
 "itself is a separate role interface over those reads, so an operator screen can never press the customer's buttons.",
 sect(src, "    MachineState state()", "    // ---------------- the two private helpers") + "\n" + X("operator console", "persistence")),

("Where does time come from, and how do you test happy hour at midnight?", "design", 5,
 "The machine is handed a <code>Clock</code>, a one-method interface, and the price rule reads that clock rather than "
 "the wall. A test hands in a clock that returns a fixed moment, asks for the price at ten in the morning, moves the "
 "same variable to half past four and asks again. The hold desk uses the same clock for expiry, which is why the "
 "expiry test moves time forward instead of sleeping. Nothing in this codebase calls <code>System.currentTimeMillis</code> "
 "except the default the machine starts with.",
 T("// 8. the price comes from", "        System.out.println(failed == 0")),

("Which pattern is where, and why did each one earn its place?", "design", 5,
 "None was chosen up front; each is what a move produced. Strategy is move 3: the price, the change algorithm and the "
 "motor are all things that will change, so each sits behind a one-method interface the machine is handed. Decorator "
 "is happy hour: a rule that wraps the old rule instead of replacing it. Observer is move 4's rule that a screen must "
 "not be inside the lock: the machine announces what happened to whoever subscribed. State is move 6: a table of "
 "modes to the actions they allow, checked before every action, so an illegal button does nothing. Singleton is "
 "absent on purpose &mdash; panels are handed the machine, so a test builds a fresh one. Factory earns its place only "
 "when columns come from a planogram file.",
 "// Strategy: a rule behind an interface, handed in\ninterface ChangeStrategy { List<Coin> plan(Map<Coin, Integer> available, int amountPaise); }\nvoid configure(PricingStrategy p, ChangeStrategy c, Dispenser d) { pricing = p; changeRule = c; motor = d; }\n\n// Decorator: wrap the old rule, add to it\nnew HappyHourDiscount(new ListPrice(), clock, 16, 18, 20);   // base.priceOf(s) minus 20% inside the window\n\n// Observer: the machine announces; it does not know what a screen is\ninterface MachineObserver { void onEvent(MachineEvent e); }\npublish(event);            // AFTER the unlock, each listener in its own try/catch\n\n// State: the modes and what each allows, as a table\nALLOWED.put(MachineState.DISPENSING, EnumSet.noneOf(Action.class));   // the flap is open: nothing is legal\n"),

("Which SOLID letter is where in this code?", "design", 5,
 "S: each class has one reason to change &mdash; a slot tracks its count, the coin box the coins, the tray what is "
 "waiting, the machine the flow and the lock, a price class the money. O: happy hour and the DP change maker were "
 "each a new class and one changed line; the machine did not open. L: any change rule drops in and the machine never "
 "checks which one it got. I: the five interfaces have one method each, so a fake is a lambda &mdash; a jammed motor "
 "is <code>code -&gt; false</code>. D: the machine depends on the interfaces it is handed in <code>configure</code>, "
 "not on <code>BackoffChange</code>, which is exactly why the tests can hand it a fixed clock and a broken motor.",
 "// S: one reason to change each\nfinal class Slot { /* its count */ }   final class CoinBox { /* the coins */ }   final class Tray { /* what is waiting */ }\n// O: new behaviour = new class, one changed line, nothing opened\nvm.configure(new HappyHourDiscount(new ListPrice(), clock, 16, 18, 20), new DpChange(), new Motor());\n// L: any implementation drops in; the machine never checks the concrete type\nList<Coin> change = changeRule.plan(coinBox.poolWith(escrow), paid - price);\n// I: one method per interface\ninterface Dispenser { boolean drop(String slotCode); }\n// D: depend on the interface, hand in the implementation; tests hand in fakes\nvm.setClock(() -> at(16, 30));   vm.configure(new ListPrice(), new BackoffChange(), code -> false);\n"),

("An enum of modes, or a class per mode? And where would a Factory pay?", "design", 3,
 "An enum plus a table, until a mode grows behaviour of its own. Four modes whose entire job is \"which buttons are "
 "legal here\" are data: one row each, and a new mode is one more row with nothing edited. The moment a mode has to "
 "remember something &mdash; out of service knowing which mode to hand back to, and still allowing the coin return so "
 "a paid customer is never stranded &mdash; promote that row to a class that answers for itself. Factory pays when "
 "columns stop being typed in code and start arriving as lines in a planogram file: one parser instead of a growing "
 "switch.",
 X("a mode with behaviour of its own", "columns from config") + "\n" + X("columns from config", "Runs every extension")),
]

